      "url": "https://github.com/faviann/dotfiles/issues/73",
      "author": "faviann",
      "title": "Stage a disabled supervised herdr unit",
      "body": "## Parent\n\n#65\n\n## What to build\n\nCreate the final supervised herdr server unit shape without activating it. This prefactor establishes the supported foreground service contract and its behavioral coverage while leaving the currently running detached herdr server and its panes untouched until the scheduled cutover ticket.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] The rendered workstation configuration contains a user service that executes the supported foreground herdr server entrypoint directly.\n- [ ] The service uses the simple service type, an absolute executable reference, a deterministic working directory, and an explicit process PATH suitable for pane and agent processes.\n- [ ] The service restarts on failure with a bounded retry delay.\n- [ ] Normal termination is left to herdr's graceful signal handling; no daemon flag, PID file, notification type, or mandatory custom stop command is introduced.\n- [ ] The unit is not enabled in a user target and is not started by applying this ticket.\n- [ ] Applying the change leaves the existing detached herdr server and active panes untouched.\n- [ ] Rendered behavioral coverage proves both the final unit semantics and its deliberately disabled staging state.\n- [ ] The workstation operating documentation explains that activation is deferred to the cutover ticket because server shutdown ends pane processes.\n- [ ] The focused dotfiles suite and nix flake check pass.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Stopping the current herdr daemon.\n- Enabling the service at boot.\n- Declaring or changing Collie's generated service.\n- Automatically updating herdr.\n\n## Blocked by\n\n- https://github.com/faviann/homelab-iac/issues/95\n\n"
    },
    {
      "kind": "referenced_parent_spec",
      "authority": "trusted-referenced-parent-spec",
      "repository": "faviann/dotfiles",
      "number": 65,
      "url": "https://github.com/faviann/dotfiles/issues/65",
      "author": "faviann",
      "title": "Add secure Collie PWA access to workstation herdr",
      "body": "## Problem Statement\n\nThe operator relies on herdr as a must-keep workstation capability but cannot comfortably inspect and control its agents from an Android phone. SSH exposes the terminal UI, but it is awkward for mobile replies, special keys, images, and notifications. Collie provides the desired PWA, but its default Tailscale deployment does not match the existing homelab architecture.\n\nThe workstation and homelab already provide WireGuard access, Traefik HTTPS ingress, Authentik forward authentication, user-service supervision, and selected persistent state. Collie must fit those boundaries without exposing terminal-equivalent access directly to the LAN or public internet, duplicating ownership of Collie's generated service, or introducing an unnecessary Tailscale dependency.\n\n## Solution\n\nProvide Collie at the single canonical origin `https://collie.admin.faviann.com`. Android clients reach that origin from the LAN or existing WireGuard network. Traefik terminates HTTPS, restricts source networks, and requires the existing Authentik `admins` role. Only the Traefik portal may reach the workstation origin port.\n\nOn the workstation, a socket-activated forwarder accepts portal traffic on port 8788 and forwards it to Collie's loopback-only bridge on port 8787. Collie communicates with the co-located herdr server through its local socket. Tailscale is not installed or used.\n\nRoll the capability out as a manual Collie pilot on top of declaratively managed prerequisites and ingress. After the full Android PWA acceptance suite passes, promote herdr boot supervision and herdr/Collie state persistence into the permanent workstation contract. Collie continues to own its generated service, plugin checkout, mutable configuration, and manual update lifecycle.\n\n## User Stories\n\n1. As the workstation operator, I want to install Collie on my Android phone, so that herdr feels like a mobile application instead of a terminal UI squeezed into SSH.\n2. As the workstation operator, I want to see which agent needs attention, so that I can respond while away from my computer.\n3. As the workstation operator, I want to read agent output from my phone, so that I can understand progress without opening a laptop.\n4. As the workstation operator, I want to reply using a normal phone text field, so that mobile typing and voice dictation remain usable.\n5. As the workstation operator, I want access to terminal special keys, so that I can interrupt, navigate, and control panes from the phone.\n6. As the workstation operator, I want to upload images from my phone, so that agents can receive screenshots and camera-roll content.\n7. As the workstation operator, I want push notifications when an agent blocks or finishes, so that I do not need to poll the dashboard.\n8. As the workstation operator, I accept agent-message content in notifications, so that alerts contain enough context to act on them.\n9. As the workstation operator, I want Collie served over HTTPS, so that Android can install it as a PWA and Web Push can operate in a secure context.\n10. As the workstation operator, I want one stable Collie origin, so that service-worker state, installation state, and push subscriptions do not fragment across URLs.\n11. As the workstation operator, I want Collie accessible anywhere through my existing WireGuard connection, so that I do not need another phone VPN client.\n12. As the workstation operator, I want LAN clients to reach the canonical Collie URL, so that the same origin works at home and remotely.\n13. As the workstation operator, I want non-LAN and non-WireGuard sources rejected, so that public DNS does not make Collie publicly usable.\n14. As the workstation operator, I want Authentik authentication in addition to the network boundary, so that possession of network access is not sufficient for terminal control.\n15. As the workstation operator, I want the existing Authentik `admins` role to authorize Collie, so that access follows the established admin-tier policy.\n16. As the workstation operator, I want authentication to fail closed, so that an Authentik outage never activates a direct or bypass path.\n17. As the workstation operator, I want an expired Authentik session to be recoverable from the installed PWA, so that the service worker cannot trap me behind a stale application shell.\n18. As the workstation operator, I want only the Traefik portal to reach Collie's workstation origin, so that LAN clients cannot bypass Authentik by using the backend port.\n19. As the workstation operator, I want Collie to remain loopback-only on its own bridge port, so that the reverse proxy remains the sole front door.\n20. As the workstation operator, I want Collie co-located with herdr under the same Unix user, so that it can use herdr's protected local socket.\n21. As the workstation operator, I want Collie to start and recover independently when herdr is temporarily unavailable, so that service ordering does not create a fragile boot sequence.\n22. As the workstation operator, I want herdr to start automatically after reboot, so that Collie does not remain disconnected until an interactive login launches herdr.\n23. As the workstation operator, I want herdr restarted after crashes but left stopped after an intentional stop, so that supervision respects operator intent.\n24. As the workstation operator, I want herdr shutdown to save its session state gracefully, so that reconstructive restore remains available after reboot.\n25. As the workstation operator, I want the one-time supervised-service cutover delayed until important panes are idle, so that adopting systemd does not unexpectedly terminate active processes.\n26. As the workstation operator, I want herdr configuration, session snapshots, plugin registration, Collie configuration, VAPID keys, and push subscriptions to survive intentional workstation LXC rebuilds, so that the mobile capability is recoverable.\n27. As the workstation operator, I accept that live pane processes cannot survive LXC destruction, so that persistence targets reconstructive state rather than impossible process continuity.\n28. As the workstation operator, I want the Collie configuration containing VAPID keys readable only by my Unix user, so that the private signing key is not exposed to other local users.\n29. As the workstation operator, I want Collie updates to remain deliberate and manual during the pilot, so that upstream changes do not silently alter remote-terminal behavior.\n30. As the workstation operator, I want infrastructure changes declared before the pilot, so that security-sensitive routes and firewall rules are never untracked live drift.\n31. As the workstation operator, I want Collie's application lifecycle owned by Collie, so that its start, restart, update, and uninstall actions remain compatible with upstream behavior.\n32. As the workstation operator, I want dotfiles to own workstation packages, herdr supervision, and the origin forwarder, so that rebuilds restore stable workstation prerequisites.\n33. As the workstation operator, I want homelab IaC to own Traefik routing, source restrictions, origin firewalling, and persistent-home mappings, so that host-level networking and storage remain in their established control plane.\n34. As the workstation operator, I want the pilot validated end to end before permanent promotion, so that a must-keep workstation contract is based on observed behavior.\n35. As the workstation operator, I want automated configuration-contract coverage in both repositories, so that later refactors cannot silently remove authentication, origin isolation, supervision, or persistence.\n\n## Implementation Decisions\n\n- The canonical browser origin is `https://collie.admin.faviann.com`; no secondary raw-port or alternate-host user path is supported.\n- Tailscale and Headscale are not part of this solution. Collie runs in its reverse-proxy mode and must not attempt to manage a Tailscale ingress.\n- Android Chrome is the initial supported installation client for the pilot.\n- The request path is Android PWA to Traefik over HTTPS, Traefik to the workstation origin forwarder, the forwarder to Collie's loopback bridge, and Collie to herdr's local socket.\n- Traefik uses the existing `websecure` entrypoint and existing wildcard certificate coverage for the admin domain.\n- The Collie router composes the existing local/VPN source restriction with the existing Authentik protected-edge forward-auth middleware.\n- The Authentik admin wildcard provider and its `admins` role are sufficient authorization. Do not add Collie's optional device gate or a separate Collie-specific Authentik application for V1.\n- Authentik failure must fail closed. There is no unauthenticated direct-origin fallback.\n- Add a higher-priority authentication recovery path under `/auth/` that reaches or redirects into the existing Authentik flow without being captured by Collie's service worker.\n- Normalize the phone WireGuard source network to `10.200.196.0/24` in relevant source policies; correct the inconsistent `12.200.196.0/24` Authentik policy entry rather than preserving it.\n- Collie's bridge retains its upstream loopback bind on port 8787.\n- A dotfiles-managed socket-activated user forwarder listens on workstation port 8788 and proxies to the Collie bridge. Reuse the established workstation loopback-plus-forwarder operating pattern.\n- The homelab firewall allows portal-origin traffic to port 8788 and rejects other non-loopback sources on that origin port. LAN clients reach Collie through Traefik, never through port 8788.\n- The Traefik upstream targets the workstation's internal inventory hostname on port 8788 and preserves the public host/origin contract expected by Collie's same-origin checks.\n- Bun is a declarative workstation package supplied by the pinned Nix/Home Manager environment.\n- Install Collie manually through herdr for the pilot. Keep its plugin checkout and update lifecycle app-owned.\n- Collie's mutable environment sets reverse-proxy mode, the exact public host, exact allowed origin, public URL, and an explicit Collie state directory.\n- Enable optional Web Push, generate a VAPID keypair, keep the private key only in Collie's mutable environment, and set that environment file to mode `0600`.\n- VAPID keys are not committed to Git or initially backed up to Bitwarden. Loss of the persistent volume may require key regeneration and phone re-enrolment.\n- Collie owns and may rewrite or delete its generated `collie` user service. Do not also declare that service in Home Manager.\n- Existing user lingering provides boot-time user-manager availability for Collie's enabled service and the later herdr service.\n- Collie is allowed to start before herdr. Its documented disconnected-and-retry behavior is the desired loose ordering contract; do not add a hard service dependency between them.\n- After pilot acceptance, declare a foreground `herdr server` user service using `Type=simple`, an absolute executable reference, an explicit useful process `PATH`, `Restart=on-failure`, and activation under the normal user target.\n- Do not use a daemon flag, PID file, notification service type, mandatory custom stop command, or `Restart=always` for herdr.\n- Migrate the currently detached herdr daemon with a one-time graceful stop followed by service activation. Schedule this only after important panes are idle because server shutdown ends pane processes.\n- Persist the herdr configuration/session/plugin subtree and the explicit Collie state subtree across intentional workstation LXC rebuilds. Do not persist the generated Collie service as an independent artifact; it can contain checkout-specific paths and must remain regenerable by Collie.\n- Herdr's reproducible agent-detection cache is not required persistent state.\n- Collie updates and herdr updates remain manual for V1 and stay outside the coordinated workstation update unit.\n- Rollout is staged: declarative Bun/forwarder and declarative ingress/firewall first; manual Collie install and full PWA pilot second; herdr supervision and persistent-state promotion only after acceptance.\n- The dotfiles issue is the umbrella tracker. Related homelab-IaC commits should reference it rather than creating an independent product specification.\n\n## Testing Decisions\n\n- Test external rendered behavior rather than individual Nix attributes, YAML line placement, shell implementation details, or generated unit text where a higher configuration seam exists.\n- Use two primary seams: an automated rendered-configuration contract across the two repositories, and a manual end-to-end acceptance pass at the canonical Android PWA origin.\n- In dotfiles, add or extend a behavioral configuration contract that proves the rendered workstation provides Bun, the Collie origin forwarder with the correct listen/upstream ports, and—after promotion—the supported herdr service command, restart policy, environment, and user-target activation.\n- Reuse the repository's declared focused-suite workflow during iteration and `nix flake check` as the sole full closeout command.\n- In homelab-IaC, extend the existing external-service contract coverage to prove the Collie hostname, HTTPS entrypoint, source restriction, Authentik middleware, canonical upstream, host/origin forwarding expectations, and `/auth/` recovery routing.\n- Extend the existing workstation baseline/firewall contract coverage to prove that the Collie origin port is allowed from portal and rejected from other sources while existing workstation services retain their policy.\n- Extend the existing persistent-home contract coverage to prove that herdr and Collie state are included without accidentally persisting the generated Collie service or reproducible cache state.\n- Cover the normalized WireGuard CIDR and removal of the incorrect CIDR through the highest existing Authentik/source-policy contract seam.\n- Run homelab-IaC's focused unit tests during iteration and its repository-prescribed unchanged aggregate regression/lint closeout before handoff.\n- The manual acceptance pass must prove that Android Chrome installs Collie as a PWA over HTTPS and that the installed application opens at the canonical origin.\n- The manual acceptance pass must prove that an Authentik admin can read panes, reply, use special keys, and upload an image.\n- The manual acceptance pass must prove that a non-admin cannot pass the Authentik boundary.\n- The manual acceptance pass must prove that a client outside the LAN/WireGuard source ranges cannot reach Collie despite public DNS resolution.\n- The manual acceptance pass must prove that a LAN client cannot bypass Authentik through the workstation origin port and that only portal can reach that port.\n- The manual acceptance pass must prove that signing out or expiring authentication exposes a usable `/auth/` recovery path from the installed PWA.\n- The manual acceptance pass must prove that Collie's push test reaches the Android phone and that tapping a notification returns to the appropriate PWA origin.\n- The manual acceptance pass must prove that Collie remains available in a disconnected state when herdr is absent and reconnects without restarting Collie after herdr starts.\n- Before permanent promotion, verify the Collie service returns after a reboot under existing user lingering.\n- After the scheduled herdr service cutover, verify graceful stop/start, crash restart behavior, intentional-stop behavior, boot activation, session snapshot restore, and Collie reconnection.\n- After persistence promotion, validate the intentional-rebuild contract for herdr configuration/session/plugin state, Collie environment/VAPID material, and push-subscription state. Live pane-process survival is not an acceptance criterion.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Tailscale, Headscale, Tailscale Serve, Tailscale Funnel, or a second phone VPN client.\n- Public Collie access with Authentik as the only boundary; LAN or WireGuard source access remains mandatory.\n- Bare router port forwarding or a direct browser path to the workstation origin port.\n- Multi-user Collie operation or authorization for roles other than Authentik `admins`.\n- Per-device authorization through Collie's device-header gate.\n- A dedicated Collie Authentik provider/application or native OIDC integration.\n- Replacing Traefik, Authentik, WireGuard, or the existing workstation persistent-home mechanism.\n- Managing Collie's generated service through Home Manager.\n- Automatic Collie or herdr updates through workstation maintenance.\n- Persisting live processes or guaranteeing active pane continuity across server stop, host reboot, or LXC destruction.\n- Backing up VAPID keys to Bitwarden in V1.\n- Supporting iOS or validating every Android browser during the pilot.\n- High availability, multiple Collie hosts under one URL, or multi-machine herd aggregation.\n- Changing Collie upstream code unless validation reveals an unavoidable compatibility defect.\n\n## Further Notes\n\n- Herdr is currently installed and running as an auto-detached server, but it has no boot-managed user service. Upstream explicitly supports `herdr server` for supervised or service-style setups.\n- The supervised herdr command stays in the foreground, handles normal termination gracefully, removes owned sockets, and saves a snapshot. A normal service restart is reconstructive and ends pane processes; it is not transparent live-process preservation.\n- Collie safely starts without a reachable herdr socket, serves a disconnected state, retries, and reconnects later. This makes loose service ordering preferable to a hard dependency.\n- The current workstation already uses the intended loopback application plus socket-forwarder pattern for another dashboard, so Collie should deepen that existing operating model rather than introduce a new proxy technology.\n- The current portal already provides wildcard certificates, admin-domain Authentik callbacks, protected-edge forward-auth, static external-service routing to the workstation, and LAN/VPN source restrictions.\n- Public wildcard DNS already resolves the Collie hostname to the homelab edge. The source allowlist and WireGuard routing—not DNS secrecy—form the network access boundary.\n- The present persistent-home inventory does not include herdr or Collie state, so intentional LXC rebuild recovery requires explicit promotion after the pilot.\n- The confirmed testing strategy has exactly two top-level seams: automated rendered deployment contracts and manual Android PWA acceptance.\n"
    }
  ],
  "omitted_untrusted_comment_count": 0,
